The subject describes only part of this commit. It also carries the European
boards' map work — the 473/468/468-line diffs on zurich/cologne/stuttgart.html
— which a concurrent session's `git add -A` swept in from another working
session. Recorded here rather than by rewriting a pushed commit.

WHAT THE MAP WORK CHANGED

Before: Zurich, Cologne and Stuttgart were the only maps on the site that drew
no vehicles — four static station dots and nothing else. All six departure
cards shared a single `transitLayer`, so a card's "show on board" checkbox
could not hide its own system's map layer, and `intlCard` pointed at layers
that were never created. Each board also still carried Amtrak: `fetchAmtrak()`
had no callers anywhere, the legend advertised an Amtrak route line and the
footer read "live — Amtrak" in Switzerland.

After: one vehicle layer and one route-line layer per departure family, created
from FAMILY_CARDS. Vehicles are placed by interpolating each trip's stop
sequence along the feed's real polyline geometry and glided between fixes by
the existing `updateFleet`, so they carry label chips, declutter, and
click-to-follow like every other board. Route lines are drawn one per route.
CARD_LAYERS gives each family its own pair, the KPI strip is wired to real
fleets (its `stG1` override, a stopgap from when there were no fleets to
count, is gone), and ~96 lines of dead Amtrak code were removed.

WHY locatableDepartures() EXISTS

The cards and the map do not always come from the same feed. Zurich's cards use
the Swiss source, which reports live delays but no trip id at all — 0 of 56
local departures carried one — and without a trip id there is no stop sequence
to place a vehicle on. Only long-distance trains appeared, because those arrive
via the Europe-wide source. So when the board's own departures cannot locate any
local service, the same nearby stops are asked of the Europe-wide source purely
for the map; the German boards get ids on the card feed and skip the extra call.
Zurich also searches six stops rather than the three the cards use, because the
lake piers sit just outside the three nearest and the Boats card had departures
the map could never place.

TWO THINGS THAT LOOK LIKE BUGS AND ARE NOT

Route lines are built from every looked-up trip, but vehicle markers only from
trips actually underway — so a half-hourly boat keeps its line between sailings
while showing no vessel. Trip lookups are interleaved across families before the
global cap applies; taking the first N in card order spent the whole budget on
the trains at the front of the list and left buses and boats permanently empty.

VERIFIED LIVE against the real feeds: Zurich 18 vehicles, Cologne 22,
Stuttgart 21, with route lines across every family on all three.
